WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.855
Finance has a sales table of 40 terabytes.

2
00:00:03.875 --> 00:00:06.005
Every monthly report scans all of it.

3
00:00:06.025 --> 00:00:07.790
And costs a fortune.

4
00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.090
The goal: scan gigabytes, not terabytes.

5
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.365
Three ideas do it.

6
00:00:12.385 --> 00:00:14.675
First: how you shape the tables.

7
00:00:14.695 --> 00:00:16.612
App databases normalize.

8
00:00:16.632 --> 00:00:20.110
Every fact is stored once, in many small tables.

9
00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.622
BigQuery likes the opposite.

10
00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:22.873
Storage is cheap.

11
00:00:22.893 --> 00:00:25.195
Joining huge tables is not.

12
00:00:25.215 --> 00:00:30.355
So keep the order lines inside the order row, with nested and repeated fields.

13
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:33.930
Data that is read together is stored together.

14
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.515
Second, and the big one: partitioning.

15
00:00:36.535 --> 00:00:39.700
Think of a filing cabinet with one drawer per day.

16
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.850
Ask for last week, and BigQuery opens seven drawers.

17
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.885
It never touches the rest.

18
00:00:44.905 --> 00:00:47.620
So partition on the date you filter by.

19
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.005
Clustering sorts the data inside each drawer, by up to four columns.

20
00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:55.940
Use it for columns with many values, like customer ID.

21
00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.740
The best setup uses both.

22
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.625
Partition by date, then cluster by your common filters.

23
00:01:01.645 --> 00:01:04.013
The trap: partitioning on an ID column.

24
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:06.350
Cluster on it instead.

25
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.135
Third: views.

26
00:01:08.155 --> 00:01:10.210
A normal view is a saved query.

27
00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.345
It runs again every time.

28
00:01:12.365 --> 00:01:15.920
A materialized view stores the answer, and keeps it fresh.

29
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.105
Great for repeated totals.

30
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:19.605
To change a column's type,

31
00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:23.980
rebuild the table with create or replace table, as select.

32
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.240
Recap.

33
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.190
Denormalize with nested fields.

34
00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.002
Partition on your date filter.

35
00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:31.150
Cluster on busy columns.

36
00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.687
Now make that report cheap.

37
00:01:32.707 --> 00:01:35.220
Checkpoint below.
